Enforce the Rice unary-length limit on the tail path

FLAC__bitreader_read_rice_signed_block rejects a Rice code whose unary
prefix exceeds `limit` (UINT32_MAX >> parameter, the most a residual
bounded to int32_t needs) only on its fast path, where the whole prefix is
already in the bit reader's buffer. When the prefix runs into the end of
the buffered input it is finished on the tail path, which had no check and
computed `msbs << parameter` modulo 2^32. A file could then decode to PCM
that other decoders (ffmpeg) do not produce, and decode differently
depending on where libFLAC's reads happened to end. With the check, both
paths reject such a code; no encoder emits one.

The file is included twice by bitreader.c, for the plain and the BMI2
decoders, so this covers both. Applied by scripts/vendor-libflac.sh after
it verifies the release archive's SHA-256. Upstream: not fixed in libFLAC
1.5.0 or on xiph/flac master as of 2026-11-12 (the file last changed in
00cb41ee, 2023-03-08); not yet reported.

--- a/src/libFLAC/deduplication/bitreader_read_rice_signed_block.c
+++ b/src/libFLAC/deduplication/bitreader_read_rice_signed_block.c
@@ -107,6 +107,9 @@ incomplete_msbs:
 			msbs += x;
 			x = ucbits = 0;
 
+			if(msbs > limit)
+				return false;
+
 			if(0) {
 incomplete_lsbs:
 				br->consumed_bits = 0;
